 ### 5.7. Authentication, authorization, and partial information
 
 Provide one local host login, separate player join credentials, and distinct credentials for every CYD and the box. Keep secrets out of source control, URLs retained after joining, screenshots, logs, and DTOs. Store credential hashes where appropriate and document initial provisioning and rotation. Exchange a player join token for a scoped browser session and redirect to a clean route. The host role is never granted by choosing a URL parameter.
+
+The generated host access key is replaced only during stopped/disarmed local maintenance. One persistent exclusive-create guard in the canonical owned local data directory excludes Vault startup and other local credential-mutating tasks. Acquire before application or task-owned storage starts; credential tasks never start Phoenix. Release only after orderly shutdown/resource closure. A crashed, partial or unreadable marker blocks both modes until an operator stops every Vault VM/task and explicitly removes the stale marker; no bypass or age/PID stealing is supported.
+
+Durably retain the protected replacement file before committing its digest and incremented epoch; preserve files and inspect active state after uncertain activation without activating an orphan. Restart paused and reject old host sessions. Preserve independent credentials, assignments and release history. Ordinary authorization, expiry and logout remain enforced; host-key replacement adds no rotation-vs-win transaction coupling. Local physical stopping remains possible without host login; after new login, complete the unchanged recovery procedure where required. Rotation cannot cancel a transmitted release, close uncertainty or authorize redelivery.
 
 Protect ordinary routes and LiveView mounts, then recheck action authorization at the game API. LiveView’s documentation explicitly requires security checks on mounts and events; a hidden button is not authorization.[5] Preserve CSRF protection, origin checking, and secure session configuration. Authenticate device connections according to the protocol and derive device identity from that authenticated connection, never from a claimed player ID in an action.
 
